## Formal signature

`{{predicate-name}} : {{domain}} × {{range}} → Bool`

## Characteristics

- **Asymmetric** — A previous-by-date B doesn't imply B previous-by-date A (OWL 2 RL `prp-asyp`)
- **Irreflexive** — an occurrent doesn't precede itself (OWL 2 RL `prp-irp`)
- **NOT transitive** — chains walk explicitly (pairwise-cons-list invariant)

## When to use previous-by-date

Use `previous-by-date` whenever the AUTHORING POSTURE is backward-looking — adding a new occurrent that references its predecessor (rather than the predecessor knowing about its successor).  Per `protocol/predicate_lifecycle.md` §"Edge assertion is one-sided", typically EITHER `next-by-date` is asserted at the predecessor side OR `previous-by-date` is asserted at the successor side — not both — and the other direction is derived via `:dt/inverse-of` entailment.

